pact of 'slower enterprise sales/support' for GCP.\\",\\n    \\"Quantified impact of 'complexity in very large non-Microsoft environments' for Azure.\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\"risk_awareness\\":      [\\n    \\"GCP: Onboarding can take 6-10 weeks for complex enterprise setups (delay in value realization, potential for extended shadow IT costs).\\",\\n    \\"GCP: Enterprise sales/support sometimes slower (could impact issue resolution and thus operational costs).\\",\\n    \\"GCP: Smaller enterprise footprint in traditional industries (potential for higher integration costs in specific sectors) and some gaps in legacy system integration.\\",\\n    \\"All vendors require commitment-based discounts (CUDs, Savings Plans, Reservations) to mitigate unpredictable usage billing; failure to manage these will result in higher pay-as-you-go costs.\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\"confidence\\":          0.9\\n}", "parsed_output": {"agent_id": "cfo", "agent_name": "Marcus Reed", "role": "Chief Financial Officer", "round": 1, "recommendation": "GCP", "score_per_vendor": {"AWS": 60, "Azure": 70, "GCP": 90}, "key_reasoning": ["GCP offers the lowest projected TCO, with Year 1 at ~$1.55M (with commitments) and Year 3 at $1.28M, making it the most cost-effective long-term choice for the organization.", "AWS presents unacceptable financial predictability risks, explicitly citing 'potential bill shock from unoptimized resources' and 'High egress costs ($0.08-$0.09/GB)', which directly contradicts our objective of maximizing financial predictability.", "GCP's TCO projections are more stable and transparent compared to AWS, which also has 'Enterprise Support fees not always transparent in initial quotes' and adds '10% of monthly usage', creating significant hidden cost exposure."], "strongest_argument": "GCP consistently offers the lowest TCO, projecting $1.55M in Year 1 and $1.28M in Year 3 with commitments, providing the most financially predictable and advantageous long-term solution.", "missing_information":