ope and cost.", "AWS/GCP: Absence of explicit details on liability caps and indemnification in the provided documents could be a point of contention in contractual negotiations, potentially leaving the enterprise exposed."], "missing_information": ["Specific expiry dates for all certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, PCI-DSS, GDPR, FedRAMP) for all vendors.", "Detailed terms of service credits beyond percentage (e.g., how to claim, specific conditions) for all vendors.", "Specific liability caps, exclusions, and full indemnification clauses for AWS and GCP.", "Detailed data ownership clauses for all vendors.", "Specific details on GDPR posture beyond general mention (e.g., DPA terms, sub-processor lists) for all vendors.", "Full scope of SLAs (e.g., for all services, not just S3 or regional clusters) for all vendors.", "Specific details on compliance audit rights for the customer for all vendors."], "cross_agent_signals": {"questions_for_other_agents": ["Financial: Are the potential costs from non-transparent AWS Enterprise Support fees factored into the TCO analysis?", "Financial: What are the financial implications of the lack of explicit liability caps for AWS and GCP?", "Technical: Does the proposed architecture for AWS and GCP adequately mitigate the risks associated with less explicit liability clauses?", "Technical: Are there any technical solutions to monitor and manage AWS egress costs to prevent bill shock, and how does this impact compliance with budget mandates?"], "dependencies_on_other_domains": ["Legal and Compliance teams depend on Financial analysis to understand the full cost implications of support fees and potential liability exposure.", "Legal and Compliance teams depend on Technical architecture to ensure data residency and protection requirements are met, especially for GDPR.", "Contractual terms regarding liability and indemnification directly impact financial risk exposure."]}, "confidence_per_vendor": {"AWS": 0.8, "Azure": 0.9, "GCP": 0.8