's 6-10 week onboarding for complex setups and gaps in legacy system integration could severely delay project timelines and increase adoption failure risk, especially for enterprises with diverse existing infrastructure.", "Azure's 'complexity in very large non-Microsoft environments' poses a high risk for organizations not deeply integrated into the Microsoft ecosystem, potentially leading to significant integration challenges and increased TCO.", "AWS's 'mixed feedback on support quality at scale' and 'slow response outside Premier tier' could lead to critical operational disruptions and extended resolution times for non-critical issues in large deployments."], "medium_risks": ["AWS's 'Enterprise Support fees not always transparent in initial quotes' could lead to unexpected budget overruns and 'bill shock' from unoptimized resources.", "GCP's 'enterprise sales/support sometimes slower' could impact overall project velocity and stakeholder satisfaction beyond technical issues."], "low_risks": ["AWS's requirement for 'additional configuration for advanced networking and observability' in EKS might introduce a minor learning curve but is manageable with skilled teams.", "Azure's 'good self-service portal' mitigates some support load but doesn't eliminate the need for direct support in complex scenarios."]}, "tradeoffs": ["Faster onboarding (Azure 3-6 weeks) versus potentially superior technical support reputation (GCP).", "Deep integration with existing Microsoft ecosystems (Azure) versus strong open-source alignment and lower vendor lock-in (GCP).", "Market leadership and extensive ecosystem (AWS) versus specialized strengths in analytics/AI (GCP) or hybrid capabilities (Azure)."], "red_flags": ["AWS: Enterprise Support fees not always transparent in initial quotes.", "AWS: Mixed feedback on support quality at scale; some reports of slow response outside Premier tier.", "GCP: Onboarding can take 6-10 weeks for complex enterprise setups.", "GCP: Some gaps in legacy 