 for Microsoft-centric organizations. However, costs can be higher without proper optimization and reservations, and it lacks the absolute lowest long-term TCO."}, "GCP": {"score": 85, "reasoning": "Presents the lowest Year 1 TCO with commitments and the most cost-effective long-term pricing. It also highlights efficiency gains (Autopilot) and does not explicitly list hidden costs like egress or mandatory support tiers in the provided document, suggesting better cost transparency upfront."}}, "risk_assessment": {"high_risks": ["AWS: Potential bill shock from unoptimized resources and high data egress costs ($0.08-$0.09/GB).", "AWS: Enterprise Support fees (10% of monthly usage) are not always transparent in initial quotes, leading to unexpected costs.", "Azure: Significantly higher costs are possible without proper utilization of reservations and Azure Hybrid Benefit."], "medium_risks": ["GCP: Slower enterprise sales/support response times could lead to prolonged issue resolution, indirectly increasing operational costs.", "GCP: Smaller enterprise footprint in traditional industries might result in less tailored financial solutions or support for specific legacy integration needs.", "Azure: Complexity in very large non-Microsoft environments could lead to higher integration and management costs."], "low_risks": ["AWS: Requirement for additional tooling for advanced networking could add minor, but predictable, costs."]}, "tradeoffs": ["GCP offers the cheapest Year 1 TCO ($1.55M with commitments) and the most cost-effective Year 3 TCO ($1.28M), but may have slower enterprise sales/support. AWS has a higher Year 1 TCO ($1.85M-$2.1M) but becomes competitive by Year 3 ($1.38M) at the expense of explicit hidden costs.", "AWS's pay-as-you-go model offers flexibility but can lead to unpredictable usage billing and bill shock without careful optimization, whereas commitment-based models (GCP CUDs, Azure Reservations, AWS Savings Plans) provide more predictable, lower costs a